(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-benign-skin-lesions-quiz"]={"title":"Benign Skin Lesions Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What are the four phases of wound healing, in order?","choices":["Hemostasis, proliferation, inflammation, remodeling","Hemostasis, inflammation, proliferation, remodeling","Inflammation, hemostasis, remodeling, proliferation","Proliferation, hemostasis, inflammation, remodeling"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — abnormal healing is loss of control over this balance. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What pressure and duration are specified for compression therapy of a keloid?","choices":["Forty millimetres of mercury, twenty-four hours a day, for at least three months","Fifteen millimetres of mercury, overnight only, for at least twelve months","Twenty-five millimetres of mercury, twenty-four hours a day, for at least six to twelve months","Twenty-five millimetres of mercury, twelve hours a day, for at least six weeks"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the proposed mechanism is tissue hypoxia and fibroblast degeneration. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which treatments are listed for a hypertrophic scar?","choices":["Padding, better-fitting footwear, over-the-counter keratolytic products, and referral to podiatry if the patient is diabetic","Oral propranolol or topical timolol, with corticosteroids given topically, intralesionally or orally as the alternative first line","Scissor excision, cryotherapy or electrodesiccation, generally performed for cosmesis and with no anesthesia necessary","Intralesional corticosteroid or fluorouracil, compression therapy and silicone sheeting, surgical excision, and pulsed dye laser"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the pulsed dye laser reduces erythema by reducing neovascularization. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which risk factors are listed for pilonidal cyst?","choices":["Obesity, local trauma or irritation, sedentary lifestyle, increased hair density in the natal cleft, and family history","Age over forty, cumulative sun exposure, very fair skin that always burns, tattoos with red ink, and previous skin trauma","Prolonged immobility, untreated incontinence, poor nutritional status, and impaired mobility or sensation at the site","African American, Hispanic or Asian ancestry, together with any preceding wound, burn, vaccination site or piercing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — hair density in the cleft is the distinctive one. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What should a patient be told about cherry angiomas?","choices":["They indicate a lipid disorder and warrant fasting cholesterol testing","They will fade within a year, so no intervention is needed","New lesions will likely develop and there is no way to prevent them","They are premalignant and each one should be biopsied before removal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — treatment is not necessary unless they bother the patient. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"What forms a dermatofibroma, and what size nodule results?","choices":["Eccrine duct cells proliferating near the surface, creating multiple papules of one to two millimetres","Fibroblasts in the dermis forming small dense clusters, creating a firm nodule of about half a centimetre to one centimetre","Endothelial cells proliferating rapidly in the dermis, creating a bright red papule that enlarges over months","Epithelium enclosed within the dermis filling with keratin, creating a movable nodule with a central punctum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the legs are the commonest site, then the arms. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What patient education is named for neurofibromatosis?","choices":["Sunscreen use and periodic skin self-examination, to reduce the number of new lesions","Avoiding cosmetic procedures such as ear piercing, because of the scarring risk","Maintaining good hygiene and seeking care if an abscess develops","National and regional support groups, for updates on treatment advances and for emotional support"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the only education point given for this condition. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"What happens to the sebaceous gland in sebaceous hyperplasia?","choices":["Sebum is trapped behind an obstructed duct, distending the gland into a cyst","Endothelial cells proliferate within the gland's blood supply, enlarging it","Fibroblasts cluster densely around the gland, forming a firm nodule","Sebocyte turnover slows with age, crowding the cells and enlarging the gland"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — there is no known potential for malignant transformation. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"How does a plexiform neurofibroma differ from a cutaneous one?","choices":["It is confined to the brain and spinal cord and never involves peripheral tissue","It is a pigmented macule rather than a tumour, and it grows with the child","It is a tumour in the tissue covering the nerves, occurs anywhere except brain and spinal cord, and is large and extensive","It is a discrete mass arising from a single peripheral nerve, sits just above or under the skin, and stays well circumscribed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — and it may be locally invasive. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"How often should a patient at risk of pressure injury be repositioned?","choices":["Every eight hours","Once per nursing shift","Only when the patient reports discomfort","Every two hours"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — alongside skin care, nutrition and specialty mattresses. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What is the only reliable way to diagnose a keratoacanthoma?","choices":["Clinical appearance alone","Response to a trial of cryotherapy","Biopsy","Dermoscopy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the clinical differential includes three malignancies. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What is an acrochordon, anatomically?","choices":["A localised overgrowth of fat cells within the subcutaneous tissue","A fibroepithelial pedunculated papilloma, with a narrow stalk and a broad tip","A benign neoplasm of eccrine ducts, appearing in crops around the eyes","A cystic enclosure of epithelium within the dermis, filled with keratin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — which is why it can be snipped off at the stalk. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What should a keloid-prone patient be advised to avoid?","choices":["Cosmetic procedures such as ear piercing, and body piercings generally","Sun exposure during peak hours, and tanning beds at any time","Tight footwear and walking barefoot without socks","Oral contraceptives and any medication affecting the liver"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — prevention is called the most important treatment. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Who gets cutaneous horns, and where?","choices":["Males two to one, very commonly, on the face, scalp, neck and trunk","Preterm Caucasian infants, females three to one, with a predilection for head and neck","Caucasians over fifty, equally male and female, on the head, neck and upper extremities","Females at puberty, on the eyelids and upper cheeks, in small crops of papules"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the sun-exposed sites are the face, ears and hands. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is fluctuance, and where would you find it in pilonidal disease?","choices":["A retraction of the lesion beneath the skin surface on lateral compression, found over a chronic sinus tract","A complete blanching of the lesion under direct pressure, found anywhere along the gluteal cleft","A palpable central punctum communicating with the skin surface, found over a previously healed tract","A wave-like movement or fluid shift on palpation indicating the lesion is fluid filled, found over an acute abscess"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the abscess is also warm, tender and erythematous. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"How is an infantile hemangioma diagnosed, and when is referral needed?","choices":["By dermoscopy, which distinguishes it from nevus flammeus and pyogenic granuloma reliably","Clinically; refer to a vascular anomalies specialist if in doubt","By biopsy in every case, because the lesion cannot be distinguished from a vascular malignancy","By magnetic resonance imaging in every case, to identify extracutaneous involvement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — most are diagnosed clinically, and the referral named is specifically to an appropriate and experienced vascular anomalies specialist. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What must a patient be told before electing cosmetic removal of a benign lesion?","choices":["The requirement for histopathological analysis in every case","The likelihood that the lesion will become malignant if left","The risk of pigmentary changes and the chance of recurrence","The need for lifelong surveillance of the site afterwards"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — both must be fully explained beforehand. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"What is a telangiectasia?","choices":["A rapidly growing vascular papule that arises in response to trauma or hormonal change","A permanently dilated capillary under one millimetre, blanchable","A congenital dilation of dermal capillaries through the full depth of the dermis, present at birth","An acquired proliferation of capillaries and venules forming a deep red papule under five millimetres"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — an acquired vascular lesion, single or in groups, with a central punctum. It may be primary or secondary and is associated with numerous diseases. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"What size and symptom characterise a clavus?","choices":["Well defined in shape, under one and a half centimetres, painful on direct pressure","Poorly defined in shape, larger than one and a half centimetres, usually painless","Well defined in shape, under one and a half centimetres, painful on side pressure","Poorly defined in shape, under one and a half centimetres, painful on direct pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — direct downward pressure is the manoeuvre that hurts. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What patient education is given for pilonidal disease?","choices":["Use sunscreen and perform periodic skin self-examination","Maintain good hygiene, and seek care if an abscess occurs","Avoid stretching the scar and avoid hot baths after surgery","Never cut or pull the lesion off at home, as it will bleed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — recurrence is common, so this is ongoing advice. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What is a syringoma, and when does it appear?","choices":["A benign neoplasm of eccrine ducts, appearing at puberty and more often in females","A benign neoplasm of endothelial cells, appearing in the first weeks of life and more often in females","A benign nerve sheath tumour, appearing at puberty and increasing with age","A benign enlargement of sebaceous glands, appearing with age and more often with immunosuppression"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — eccrine, meaning sweat gland. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Why is the name pyogenic granuloma described as a misnomer?","choices":["It is neither cutaneous nor mucosal, since it arises only within joints","It is neither infectious nor granulomatous","It is neither pyogenic nor benign, since most are early angiosarcomas","It is neither acquired nor vascular, since it is a congenital malformation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is an acquired benign vascular tumour. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"In whom and where do skin tags occur, and how common are they?","choices":["Increased in males two to one, on the face, the scalp, the neck and the trunk; very common and occurring at any adult age","Increased in females two to one, on the legs and the arms, and found in all races; a common lesion appearing after minor trauma","Increased in males three to one, over the coccyx and along the gluteal cleft; recurrence after treatment is common","Increased in females and obese patients, in areas of friction such as the neck, axilla and groin; present in sixty per cent of people by age seventy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — very common, and increasingly so with age. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What laboratory testing does an epidermoid cyst require?","choices":["Usually none","Culture of the expressed material in every case","Fasting lipid panel to screen for a lipid disorder","Punch biopsy of the cyst wall before any excision"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the diagnosis is clinical. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Why should biopsy of a keloid be avoided unless the diagnosis is genuinely in doubt?","choices":["Because the biopsy itself may induce new scarring","Because the specimen is almost never diagnostic in fibrous lesions","Because bleeding from a keloid is difficult to control","Because the lesion seeds along the needle track when sampled"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the diagnostic act can provoke the disease it is investigating. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What is the pathophysiology of nevus flammeus?","choices":["Dilated dermal capillaries, with no endothelial proliferation","Rapid proliferation of endothelial cells from mutations in the genes regulating that proliferation","Proliferation of capillaries and venules, acquired and increasing with age","Dilation of preexisting arterioles under the influence of excess circulating estrogen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the dilated superficial dermal capillaries run the entire depth of the dermis, and the absence of endothelial proliferation is why this lesion never involutes. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Which patient with a corn or callus needs referral, and to whom?","choices":["The diabetic patient, referred to podiatry","The elderly patient, referred to dermatology","The immunosuppressed patient, referred to infectious disease","The pregnant patient, referred to obstetrics"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the deck flags this with an exclamation mark. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What makes a pressure injury unstageable?","choices":["Full thickness skin loss in which only adipose tissue can be seen in the wound bed, with no deeper structure exposed","Persistent non-blanchable deep red or purple discolouration of skin that may be either intact or non-intact","A localised area of non-blanchable erythema over skin that is otherwise entirely intact and undamaged","Full thickness skin and tissue loss whose extent cannot be determined because it is obscured by slough or eschar"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the depth is unknowable until the cover is removed. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What are the examination findings of an epidermoid cyst?","choices":["A translucent skin-coloured papule sitting over the distal interphalangeal joint, which may groove the nail longitudinally","A single firm movable nodule with a central punctum, expressing pasty material that smells of rancid cheese","A soft painless subcutaneous nodule of rubbery consistency, usually under five centimetres across, with no overlying pore or punctum at all","A firm nodule with a hyperpigmented brown halo and a scaly surface, which retracts beneath the skin on lateral compression"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a round protruding mass whose central pore communicates with the skin surface. The punctum and the smell of the cream-coloured contents are the two giveaways. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"How is a digital mucous cyst managed?","choices":["Intralesional corticosteroid, followed by silicone sheeting over the nail","Immediate excision in all cases, because of the risk of joint infection","Observation if asymptomatic; excision if symptomatic or causing nail dystrophy","Aspiration at every visit, because the mucin reaccumulates predictably"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the nail change is itself an indication. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 108"}]};
